/*
 * {{kernel}}-oracle.c — {{stem}}'s kernel-identity test: both built faces, sample for sample, against
{{^chain}} * omx-dsp's {{kernel}} instance face called DIRECTLY on the same blocks, at every declared rate,
{{/chain}}{{#chain}} * omx-dsp's instance faces of its chain ({{sequence}}) called DIRECTLY, one after the other in the
 * order the `order` parameter picks, each with its own switch, on the same blocks, at every declared rate,
{{/chain}} * engaged and bypassed — no tolerance.
 *
 *   {{kernel}}-oracle clap <{{stem}}.clap>
 *   {{kernel}}-oracle lv2 <bundle-dir> <uri>
 *
 * GENERATED — DO NOT EDIT BY HAND. Produced by tools/gen.mjs from plugins/{{stem}}/{{stem}}.decl.json:
 * the plan moves every declared parameter across its travel between uneven blocks (1 to 3000 frames)
 * and toggles the bypass; then, timed in milliseconds so every rate gets the same time, a full-scale
 * burst and a quiet tail at the defaults (a dynamics kernel engages and releases), and each value of
 * every choice held over its own burst and tail with the other parameters stepped once (a control
 * read only under one value is reached). All of it runs through ONE instance of the face. Parameters are found BY NAME (CLAP) or BY
 * SYMBOL (LV2, through lilv), so a renumbered face cannot pass by accident. Two guards keep the test
 * honest: the reference must move the signal, and a reference with any ONE parameter one step off must
 * differ from the face (the sabotage arm), so a wrong or dead coefficient is seen. A CLAP face's
 * reported latency holds while it is active and moves only at the activation after the restart it
 * asks for, as the CLAP latency extension says. The plan runs again IN PLACE (each output the buffer of
 * its input), bit for bit the first run; and, through CLAP, with each block split in two, its second half
 * carrying no event, bit for bit the kernel resolving the same values again before that half.
{{#measuresLatency}} * The declaration's latency checks: at each, through both faces at every declared rate, a unit
 * impulse lands where the reported latency says (within one frame: the kernel's delay can be
 * fractional, the report is its rounding), or the reported latency is the number declared.
{{/measuresLatency}}{{#metered}} * The declaration's meters: after every block of the plan, through the CLAP face's clap.meters/1 and
 * the LV2 face's output control ports (one per channel, the value as a float) at every declared rate,
 * every channel of every meter is held bit for bit to a reference computed here
 * from the same block: a gain reduction to what the reference's instance holds at its declared field
 * (0 while bypassed), a level to the peak of the block's input or of the reference's output, in dBFS,
 * floored at the meter's display minimum.{{#meterKernelLevel}} A level the kernel reads is the reference instance's own
 * reading (the display minimum while bypassed).{{/meterKernelLevel}} The meters are listed as declared, every one coherent;
 * nothing is read before the first block (docs/meters.md).
{{#meterGr}} * The plan must make the kernel reduce gain.
{{/meterGr}}{{#meterLevel}} * The plan must move the levels: the two legs differ in some block{{#meterIn}}{{#meterOut}}, and the input and the
 * output differ in some block{{/meterOut}}{{/meterIn}}.
{{/meterLevel}}{{/metered}}{{#held}} * Held at its default in every block, because changing it re-arms the kernel's state (omx-contract
 * `rearms`): {{symbol}}.
{{/held}}{{#monitors}} * Held at its default where the others move or are stepped, a monitor (it replaces the output while on),
 * with its own section still: {{symbol}}.
{{/monitors}}{{#engaged}} * Held at {{value}} in the release and choice sections, never stepped there (engaged): {{symbol}}.
{{/engaged}}{{#keyed}} *
 * KEYED: the face listens to a sidechain. The plan runs twice per rate: with a key that differs from
 * the main signal (the sabotage arm runs here), and with no key routed (NULL: the kernel's own
 * detector). A reference keyed by that key must differ from the unkeyed one, or the key is ignored.
{{/keyed}}{{#tempo}} *
 * TEMPO: the face reads the host's tempo. Every block hands it ORACLE_BPM, the way a host does (CLAP: the
 * transport's tempo, CLAP_TRANSPORT_HAS_TEMPO; LV2: a time:Position with time:beatsPerMinute on `control`),
 * and the reference is handed the same, so a synced time is compared as every other control is.
{{/tempo}} */
